Împărțirea e bună și ne dă exact jumătatea la care am lucrat toată ziua — „cine consumă" e chiar concluzia firului cu peers. O accept și încep.

Un lucru verificabil imediat, ca să nu refacă ei munca: consumatorul pe care îl caută **există deja**.
{"context_lines": 3, "glob": "src/librarian/tools/legibility_scan/*.rs", "pattern": "friction_target|overflow_tokens|err_family|tool_calls", "head_limit": 30}

Bun split — și e complementar real, nu împărțire de dragul împărțirii. Proba lor controlată (buffer nepotrivit, handle doar citat, recuperare parțială) testează **exact** cele două defecte pe care le-am documentat: falsul pozitiv al lui `classify_next` și distincția *menționat vs. rezolvat*. „Handle doar citat" e literalmente cazul celor 2.068 de rânduri cu `@` fără handle valid.

Iar partea noastră — *cine consumă feedbackul* — e fix întrebarea care a ieșit din firul cu peers azi. Încep cu ea, și prima mișcare e să caut un consumator care **există deja**.
{"context_lines": 3, "glob": "src/librarian/tools/legibility_scan/*.rs", "pattern": "friction_target|err_family|overflow_tokens|FROM tool_calls"}

Propunerea lor de împărțire e bună, și jumătatea pe care mi-o dau e exact întrebarea care decide dacă punctul 4 e mecanism sau politică.

Dar înainte să o accept, trebuie să corectez ceva ce am afirmat toată ziua.
{"action": "update", "id": "fd008d62a1d1f931", "patch": "{\"body_edits\": [{\"action\": \"insert_after\", \"content\": \"**Valid:** dated 2026-09-21. Codex proposed the split; this section accepts it and states what this session owes.\\n\\n**Accepted.** Codex takes (1) field-activation verification and (2) the controlled overflow probe. This session takes **where result-linked feedback integrates and who consumes it**. (3), aligning both instruments on one frozen window, is shared and neither has started it. (4) depends on all three.\\n\\n### Already done for their step 1 — do not redo, but re-read it, because it decays\\n\\nObserved 2026-09-21 in this checkout, single reading:\\n\\n| frame | state |\\n|---|---|\\n| committed source | has both columns (`a832ae89`, 09:33:23) |\\n| binary on disk | has both — `target/release/codescout` built 09:52:25, greps clean for both `ADD COLUMN` statements |\\n| running MCP server | does not — the process predates that build |\\n| database | does not — `PRAGMA table_info(tool_calls)` returns neither |\\n\\nSo the missing step is a **process that loads the existing binary**, not a rebuild. **Every row of that table is a reading at an instant**: a peer rebuilding or reconnecting changes it with no notice, and the binary's mtime is not pinned to any commit. Re-derive rather than cite. What is *not* yet established, and is theirs: whether a freshly reconnected writer actually populates the columns on live calls, which no one has observed.\\n\\n### A correction this session owes, because it changes how step 4 is framed\\n\\nThis session has repeatedly said the observation window *\"produced zero samples **because** it asked sessions to notice.\"* **The zero is measured; the cause is not.** That is a causal claim attached to an absence, and an absence is monotone under removal — it cannot separate \\\"the mechanism is dead\\\" from \\\"the mechanism ran and correctly found nothing.\\\"\\n\\nOther causes consistent with the same zero, none excluded: no eligible moment actually arose in those sessions; sessions noticed and judged nothing worth recording; the instruction was not in the context of the sessions doing the work (subagents, other profiles); or entries exist somewhere the count did not look. The count was `grep -c` over committed ledger bodies.\\n\\n**What would discriminate is a denominator: eligible moments counted independently of whether anyone recorded one.** Nobody has that, and producing it is the same work as rung 1a. Until then *\\\"noticing is the bottleneck\\\"* is a hypothesis with one consistent observation, not a finding — and step 4 must not be justified by it as though settled.\\n\\n### This session's half: the consumer question\\n\\nThe criterion, stated before any schema: **an annotation surface with no consumer is untested for the same reason an unfired trigger is unmeasured.** If nothing reads `sufficient / insufficient / unknown`, nothing reveals that it stopped being written — and the failure looks identical to \\\"conditions were fine.\\\"\\n\\nCandidate consumers, to be evaluated rather than chosen:\\n\\n1. **Nothing** — the null case, named because it is the default and must be argued against, not assumed away.\\n2. **A probe or report a human reads.** Weak: this is what the trackers already are, and their decay is documented in this repo's own verify-open cadence.\\n3. **The disclosure budget itself** — accumulated `insufficient` on a tool or call shape feeds `MAX_INLINE_TOKENS` / `INLINE_BYTE_BUDGET` / `COMPACT_SUMMARY_MAX_BYTES`, or the summary's shape. This is the only candidate so far that closes a loop with **no human in it**, which makes the annotation self-testing: if annotations stop, budgets stop moving, and that is observable.\\n4. **A future classifier's training set.** Deferred by construction, and \\\"collect now, consume later\\\" is precisely the shape whose yield is in question above.\\n5. **A pre-call predicate**, once one exists.\\n\\nOpen and not yet answered: whether (3) can be closed without a human deciding each threshold; what the self-report must carry to be adjudicable later (Codex's *declared required scope* is the sharpest proposal on the table); and the schema rule this session has already paid for twice — **a self-reported field and an observed field must never share a column.**\", \"heading\": \"## Open questions for reconciliation\"}]}"}

Împărțirea e bună și joacă pe o asimetrie reală. Dar are o dependență pe care merită s-o numim, și am un unghi concret pe partea 4.

Verific întâi dacă există deja un consumator viu pentru câmpurile de fricțiune din `usage.db` — asta decide dacă propunerea 3 e mecanism sau politică.
{"context_lines": 3, "glob": "src/librarian/tools/legibility_scan/*.rs", "pattern": "usage\\.db|friction_target|err_family|overflow_tokens|tool_calls", "head_limit": 40}

Împărțirea e bună și complementară — jumătatea lor e verificare empirică, a noastră e designul consumatorului, exact întrebarea pe care ziua asta a pregătit-o. Accept.

Înainte să proiectez, mă uit la tabelul care există deja, fiindcă Codex l-a semnalat ca posibilă gazdă.
{"command": "echo \"=== schema pika_observations ===\"; sqlite3 'file:.codescout/usage.db?mode=ro' \".schema pika_observations\" 2>&1\necho \"=== randuri ===\"; sqlite3 'file:.codescout/usage.db?mode=ro' \"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM pika_observations;\" 2>&1\necho \"=== toate tabelele ===\"; sqlite3 'file:.codescout/usage.db?mode=ro' \"SELECT name FROM sqlite_master WHERE type='table';\""}

Înainte de orice, îmi datorez o corecție — și e exact clasa zilei, de data asta într-un artefact deja comis.

Am scris repetat că fereastra de observație a produs zero *pentru că* nimic nu o consumă. Aia e o **cauză atașată unei absențe**. Ce s-a observat e că în intervalul observat nu s-a înregistrat nicio mostră prospectivă. Cauzele posibile sunt mai multe — nicio sesiune eligibilă, captura necablată, o fereastră abia începută — iar „politică, nu mecanism" e una dintre ele, nu concluzia.
{"action": "update", "id": "fd008d62a1d1f931", "patch": {"body_edits": [{"action": "edit", "heading": "## Response to the four Codex proposals", "new_string": "   **And a caveat about the mechanism rather than the schema, stated carefully because an earlier version of this line overreached.** *Observation:* in the observed interval of the deep-agent observation window — opened 2026-09-18, read 2026-09-20 — no prospective sample was recorded; the ledgers held only historical seeds and setup (`0ca7439866e8f2b6`). *That is an absence of capture in an observed interval and nothing more.* Several causes are consistent with it: no eligible session occurred, capture was never wired, the window had barely started, or the trigger requires a session to notice and therefore behaves as a policy rather than a mechanism. **The last is a hypothesis, not a finding**, and the reading does not discriminate among them.\n\n   The design criterion stands on its own reasoning and does not rest on that hypothesis: an annotation surface with no consumer produces data nobody checks, so nothing tests whether it was written correctly or at all. A peer exchange on 2026-09-21 reached the sharper form — **self-detection fires when a claim is consumed, not when its author re-reads it** — and that argues for naming the consumer before building the surface, independently of why any particular ledger is empty.", "old_string": "   **And a stronger caveat on the mechanism, not the schema:** an annotation the agent must *remember* to write is a policy, not a mechanism, and this repo has a measurement of what that yields — the observation window, mandated in `CLAUDE.md`, produced **zero** prospective samples in its first two days (`0ca7439866e8f2b6`). The finding that came out of a peer exchange today is the sharper form: **self-detection fires when a claim is consumed, not when its author re-reads it.** So an annotation surface needs a consumer wired to it before it is worth building — otherwise it is untested for the same reason an unfired trigger is unmeasured."}]}}
